Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Disproportionate regional pain, Allodynia/Hyperalgesia, Vasomotor asymmetry, Sudomotor/Trophic changes

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-015
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, unmanageable neuro-inflammatory pain crisis in a limb that previously sustained minor trauma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing agonizing, disproportionate pain, severe swelling, and extreme sensitivity to touch in a single limb following a sprain 6 weeks prior. Urgent assessment was strictly mandated to evaluate for deep vein thrombosis, acute compartment syndrome, or severe occult infection, and to initiate emergency neuropathic pain modulation to halt acute physiological and psychological distress before daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My foot is on fire and swollen like a balloon; even the vibration of someone walking in the room sends shockwaves of agony up my leg."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a severe pain crisis in the left lower extremity. Timeline: Sustained a minor left ankle sprain 6 weeks ago; instead of resolving, pain steadily worsened, becoming continuous and intense. Over the last 48 hours, the pain escalated dramatically, becoming completely disproportionate to the original injury. Patient reports severe mechanical allodynia (screaming when a blanket touches the foot) and hyperalgesia. Describes the limb feeling burning hot, then freezing cold. Severe swelling developed yesterday.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound, refusing any movement of the affected limb.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound psychological distress and sleep deprivation secondary to unremitting, agonizing neuropathic pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 154/96 | HR: 112 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Highly distressed, diaphoretic, hyper-vigilant, actively shielding the left leg from any environmental contact.
Physical Exam: Left lower limb demonstrates marked temperature asymmetry (noticeably warmer to light dorsal touch than the right). Distinct skin color changes present; left foot appears mottled, red, and slightly shiny compared to the right. Significant, indurated edema present from the toes to the mid-calf. Severe allodynia demonstrated: extreme pain provoked by merely brushing a cotton swab over the dorsum of the foot. Motor function heavily restricted by pain, patient refuses to actively dorsiflex or plantarflex. Pulses are palpable bilaterally (bounding on the left due to vasodilation). No signs of tracking cellulitis or localized purulent collections. Negative Homan's sign.
Environmental Observations: Room kept perfectly dark and quiet; patient expresses extreme fear of any stimulus.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Ibuprofen are completely ineffective for this neuropathic phenotype.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of suspected Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS Type I) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out clinically by the presence of severe allodynia, profound vasomotor skin changes, and bounding pulses, which are characteristic of CRPS rather than venous occlusion.

Cellulitis - Ruled out by the absence of systemic fever, lack of a primary infectious source/wound, and the presence of hyperhidrosis and allodynia, which point to autonomic/neuropathic dysfunction.

Compartment Syndrome - Ruled out by the sub-acute onset over weeks, presence of bounding pulses, and the predominantly superficial/cutaneous nature of the extreme pain rather than deep muscular ischemia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in an acute state of severe distress characterized by profound, disproportionate regional pain, allodynia, and distinct autonomic signs (vasomotor and sudomotor asymmetry) following minor trauma. This classic presentation strongly suggested CRPS. The extremity of the pain—rendering standard analgesics useless and completely immobilizing the patient—created an acute crisis. Delaying evaluation would have resulted in unmanageable psychological trauma, sleep deprivation, and the risk of missing limb-threatening vascular events. Urgent after-hours assessment safely excluded DVT and acute infection, recognized the complex neuropathic and autonomic nature of the syndrome, and permitted the immediate initiation of specialized membrane-stabilizing medications to break the acute sympathetic pain loop.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Pregabalin 75mg STAT. Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg BD ongoing. Explicitly advised against ice application (can exacerbate vasomotor dysfunction).
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Implement a "desensitization protocol" by very gently rubbing a soft silk cloth over the uninjured parts of the leg, slowly moving toward the painful area. Consequence: Gradual retraining of the brain to recognize non-threatening touch, leading to a slow reduction in the terrifying allodynia response (positive reinforcement).
Action: Keep the limb elevated slightly above the heart using soft pillows, while actively performing tiny, pain-free toe wiggles. Consequence: Pumping out the indurated edema via the muscle pump, physically reducing the tight, bursting sensation in the skin (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to a specialized Pain Medicine physician and specialized physiotherapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to titrate neuropathic medications and expedite pain clinic referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden onset of systemic fevers, pus draining from anywhere on the limb, or if the foot turns completely white, cold, and pulseless.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, utilizing the Budapest Criteria. Formal specialist evaluation is mandatory for this highly complex chronic condition. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: CRPS is a dysfunction of the nervous system where pain signals are amplified. Standard painkillers will not work. Recovery requires highly specialized neuropathic medications and intense, specialized physical therapy to "reboot" the nerves.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if you develop a fever, if your foot turns completely white and freezing cold, or if you completely lose the ability to feel a hard pinch on your toes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your nervous system has overreacted to the original sprain and is stuck in a "high alert" mode, misinterpreting normal touch as severe damage. The new medication (Pregabalin) works directly on the nerves to dampen down these false alarm signals.
Actionable Steps: Do not use ice, as extreme temperatures can make the nerve dysfunction worse. You must start gently moving the toes, even though it is scary. Total immobility will cause the limb to freeze up permanently.

